WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.100
In the first film, removing a restraint let a stress responder climb fast.

2
00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:13.500
Once p53 rises, how does a cell choose between repair and permanent arrest?

3
00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:18.660
Does heavier damage simply turn p53 up like a dimmer dial?

4
00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:23.380
To find out, researchers watched one living cell at a time.

5
00:00:24.383 --> 00:00:31.103
Grind millions of irradiated cells onto a blot, and p53 rises and fades, at most

6
00:00:31.103 --> 00:00:34.823
with a ripple. That dish average looks like a dial.

7
00:00:36.163 --> 00:00:42.023
Film single living cells instead, and p53 fires separate pulses of fixed average

8
00:00:42.023 --> 00:00:47.743
height and width. Higher doses add more pulses on average, not taller ones.

9
00:00:48.817 --> 00:00:50.737
Why did the blot hide those pulses?

10
00:00:52.117 --> 00:00:53.637
Because cells pulse out of step.

11
00:00:54.917 --> 00:00:57.997
An average equals the sum divided by the cell count.

12
00:00:59.417 --> 00:01:02.437
Suppose four cells each peak at 80, one after another.

13
00:01:03.717 --> 00:01:09.077
At any hour, 80 plus 0 plus 0 plus 0, divided by 4, equals 20.

14
00:01:09.957 --> 00:01:13.437
Out-of-step flashes blend into a low, smooth wave.

15
00:01:14.517 --> 00:01:17.797
Those pulses follow gamma rays that break both strands.

16
00:01:19.277 --> 00:01:22.177
Do cells actually read that pulsing shape over time?

17
00:01:24.037 --> 00:01:30.097
Researchers blocked MDM2 with a drug to hold p53 steady at about one pulse's height.

18
00:01:31.357 --> 00:01:34.177
Same protein, same peak, only the shape differs.

19
00:01:35.537 --> 00:01:37.177
Will the steady cells still recover?

20
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:47.800
Mostly not, which means shape over time steered fate, not peak height alone.

21
00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:53.220
In the cell line studied, pulsing cells tended to recover and divide.

22
00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:59.160
Steady cells more often entered senescence, a permanent exit from division.

23
00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:06.420
Steady p53 also meant more in total, yet at equal totals it still drove senescence

24
00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:12.100
genes harder. How a stress signal unfolds over time helps decide cell fate.

25
00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:18.680
A similar brief-versus-lasting choice happens inside the endoplasmic reticulum,

26
00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:22.280
where secreted proteins fold with chaperone helpers.

27
00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:29.080
When unfolded chains pile up, three membrane sensors wake up, partly because the

28
00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:32.040
chaperone BiP leaves to bind the unfolded chains.

29
00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:38.060
First they buy time for repair by slowing new protein entry and ordering more

30
00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:39.060
chaperones.

31
00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:45.210
If that folding backlog drags on, those same sensors shift their message toward cell

32
00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:50.910
death. One branch activates a death-promoting factor called CHOP, while another

33
00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:53.570
signals through the stress kinase JNK.

34
00:02:55.270 --> 00:02:59.890
Stress strength and cell state matter too, and repair and death signals can overlap.

35
00:03:00.983 --> 00:03:06.603
Across both pathways, brief or pulsed signals favour repair, while sustained ones

36
00:03:06.603 --> 00:03:08.123
suggest repair is failing.

37
00:03:09.443 --> 00:03:12.383
One snapshot of protein level misses half the story.

38
00:03:13.783 --> 00:03:18.323
Reading the full timeline helps steer the cell between recovery and irreversible

39
00:03:18.323 --> 00:03:18.883
fate.
